window.LIBRARY_DATA = [{"kind":"vol","code":"01","title":"Mitochondrial","text":"**Peptides, Practiced. — Beyond the Course**\n\n**Peptide Teaching Edition**\n\n*Volume 1 — Mitochondrial Peptides*\n\nSS-31 (Elamipretide)  ·  MOTS-c  ·  NAD⁺\n\nProvider Reference  |  v1.0\n\n| **How to use this volume** Each peptide entry follows a standard 12-section structure: quick stats, what it is, mechanism, indications, dosing rationale, evidence base, contraindications, drug interactions, monitoring, side effects, stacking & synergy, and clinical pearls. This is the teaching edition — full clinical depth for top-tier peptides. For quick lookup of doses or vial sizes, use Peptide_Master_Reference.docx in the same folder. *Mitochondrial peptides are the foundation of every complex chronic illness protocol. Sequence: SS-31 first → MOTS-c second → NAD⁺ third. Always.* |\n| --- |\n\n# Mitochondrial Peptides — Category Overview\n\nMitochondrial dysfunction is the converging endpoint of the chronic illness picture you treat: mold/mycotoxin exposure, Lyme and co-infections, post-viral syndromes (long COVID, post-EBV/CMV reactivation), chronic inflammation, agricultural toxin injury, and accelerated aging. The mitochondria are both target AND amplifier — once they're dysfunctional, every other system fails downstream.\n\nThe clinical phenotype is recognizable: profound fatigue out of proportion to findings, cognitive slowing, exercise intolerance with post-exertional crashes, dyspnea on exertion, muscle weakness, poor toxin tolerance, and a tendency for any therapeutic intervention to cause flares. Conventional labs are often unrevealing — these patients have normal CMPs, normal CBCs, often normal thyroid panels — yet are clearly not well.\n\nMitochondrial peptides repair this terrain in a specific sequence. Out of order, they make patients worse. The sequencing rule:\n\n| **The mitochondrial sequence — say it like a clinical mantra** 1.  SS-31 first  —  repair the inner mitochondrial membrane (cardiolipin), stop ROS leak, restore ATP capacity. This is the 'damage control' phase. Always 2–4 weeks alone before adding anything else in fragile patients. 2.  MOTS-c second  —  reactivate AMPK, restore metabolic flexibility, restart the metabolic 'software.' Do NOT start in acute illness — raises metabolic demand. 3.  NAD⁺ third  —  upgrade the operating system: sirtuin activation, DNA repair, redox balance. Pairs with both above. |\n| --- |\n\nA useful way to frame the sequence: SS-31 repairs the hardware, MOTS-c restarts the software, and NAD⁺ upgrades the operating system. Patients fail when this sequence is inverted — when MOTS-c is given before membrane integrity is restored, ROS production rises faster than antioxidant defense can match, and the patient flares. When NAD⁺ is given without sirtuin substrate availability, the methyl-donor pool depletes and patients become irritable and exhausted.\n\nAdjuncts to ALWAYS include alongside this protocol: NAC (600–1200 mg/day), alpha-lipoic acid (300–600 mg/day), CoQ10 (200–400 mg/day, ubiquinol form preferred in older patients), liposomal glutathione (500 mg/day), magnesium glycinate (300–600 mg/day), B-complex with methylated forms. Without the antioxidant network and B-vitamin support, the peptides cannot do their work — they're tools, not magic.\n\n| **When to pause mitochondrial peptides** During acute illness (viral flares, bacterial infections, Herx reactions, surgery, trauma), the goal shifts from BUILDING energy to PROTECTING mitochondria. Pause MOTS-c and NAD⁺. Keep SS-31 going if tolerated — it's protective, not stimulating. Resume the full protocol once acute illness resolves and CRP normalizes. |\n| --- |\n\n| **SS-31 (Elamipretide)**  —  *Cardiolipin-targeted mitochondrial membrane repair peptide* |\n| --- |\n| **Vial** | **Dose** | **Route** | **Cycle** |\n| 10 mg / 1 mL  (also 40 mg / 3 mL — PHR Pharmacy) | 5–10 mg/day | Subcutaneous | 22 days (11 vials) standard; or 5 days/week × 3–8 weeks |\n\n### What it is\n\nSS-31 (also known as MTP-131, elamipretide, or Bendavia) is a synthetic 4-amino-acid aromatic-cationic peptide (D-Arg-2'6'-dimethylTyr-Lys-Phe-NH2) developed by Hazel Szeto and Peter Schiller in the early 2000s. It belongs to the Szeto-Schiller (SS) family of peptides designed to selectively target the inner mitochondrial membrane. Unlike most peptides, SS-31 does not bind a surface receptor — it concentrates 1,000–5,000-fold in the mitochondrial inner membrane, where it interacts directly with cardiolipin, the signature phospholipid of the cristae. It is currently in Phase 3 trials for Barth syndrome and primary mitochondrial myopathy under the trade name Elamipretide (sponsored by Stealth BioTherapeutics).\n\n### Mechanism of action\n\n- Selectively binds and stabilizes cardiolipin in the inner mitochondrial membrane — the phospholipid that anchors and organizes electron transport chain (ETC) complexes I, III, IV, and ATP synthase.\n\n- Restores cristae curvature and membrane potential, which are lost when cardiolipin is oxidized by ROS in stressed mitochondria.\n\n- Reduces electron leak from ETC complexes I and III, dramatically lowering reactive oxygen species (ROS) production at the source.\n\n- Restores oxidative phosphorylation efficiency and ATP output — ATP rises typically 30–80% in dysfunctional mitochondria.\n\n- Inhibits cytochrome c peroxidase activity (cardiolipin's pathologic conversion when oxidized), preventing apoptotic cascade initiation.\n\n- Restores mitophagy and autophagy signaling — damaged mitochondria can finally be cleared and replaced.\n\n- Indirectly improves NAD⁺/NADH ratio by restoring ETC function, supporting downstream sirtuin activity.\n\n### Clinical indications\n\n- Mold/mycotoxin illness and CIRS — foundational mitochondrial repair before any other therapy\n\n- Long COVID and post-viral fatigue syndromes\n\n- Chronic Lyme and co-infection-driven fatigue\n\n- Heart failure with preserved or reduced ejection fraction (HFpEF/HFrEF) — improves diastolic function in clinical trials\n\n- Mitochondrial myopathies (primary genetic) — Barth syndrome, MELAS, MERRF\n\n- Age-related macular degeneration (Phase 1 ReCLAIM data showed visual improvement)\n\n- Sarcopenia and exercise intolerance in aging\n\n- Ischemia-reperfusion injury (cardiac, cerebral)\n\n- Hearing loss (animal data showing protection from ototoxic insults)\n\n- Pre-conditioning for cancer chemotherapy (research stage)\n\n- Renal protection in contrast-induced nephropathy\n\n- Pre-treatment before MOTS-c, NAD⁺, GH peptides, or any 'metabolic activator' in fragile patients\n\n### Dosing rationale & timing\n\nThe 5 mg/day dose evolved from human cardiac trials where this dose produced consistent ATP and ejection-fraction improvement. The 22-day continuous protocol comes from sustained-dosing studies showing maximal cardiolipin restoration at ~3 weeks. Higher doses (10 mg/day) are used in severe mitochondrial disease, advanced CIRS, or failed response to 5 mg. Subcutaneous absorption is rapid; IV is used in some hospitalized contexts but offers no advantage for outpatient use. Time of day does not significantly affect efficacy — most clinicians use morning or pre-workout dosing for subjective energy benefit. Food restriction is unnecessary. The peptide should be refrigerated; reconstituted vials are stable 28 days. For maintenance after the 22-day cycle, 5 mg three times weekly maintains the membrane-stabilizing effect.\n\n| **Optional — easing in for the unusually sensitive patient** **This is a judgment call, not a default. **For most patients, 5 mg/day is the right starting dose. But in the occasional highly reactive patient — someone whose system is stuck in a deep protective, hypometabolic “danger” state and who flares on almost any intervention — you have the option to ease in below 5 mg. You know your patients; use this only when their sensitivity warrants it. **If you choose to ease in — a reasonable on-ramp:** •  2.5 mg every other day for about a week (most reactive patients) — assess tolerance. •  2.5 mg daily for 1–2 weeks. •  5 mg daily — the standard therapeutic dose; hold here 2–4 weeks alone before adding MOTS-c or any metabolic activator. •  10 mg/day — reserved for advanced CIRS, severe mitochondrial dysfunction, or a failed response at 5 mg. **Why a lower dose still gives signal: **SS-31 concentrates in the inner mitochondrial membrane at roughly 1,000–5,000 times the surrounding cytosol, and it works by binding and stabilizing cardiolipin — a high-affinity, target-saturating repair mechanism, not a linear dose-response like a metabolic stimulant. A fraction of the standard dose therefore still delivers meaningful cardiolipin occupancy and reduces ROS at the source. Easing in doesn’t sacrifice the repair signal — it simply lets you confirm the patient is stabilizing rather than reacting before moving to the full dose. **Reconciling this with the old caution: **Clinicians are traditionally taught to avoid mitochondrial therapies in these danger-state patients, because stimulating mitochondrial output while the cell is still under threat and flooded with ROS drives more electron flux through a leaky chain, generates more ROS, and deepens the danger signal. That caution is correct — but it applies to stimulators (metabolic activators, aggressive biogenesis agents, and within this category, MOTS-c). SS-31 is not a stimulator. It repairs and stabilizes the very membrane lesion the danger state is generating, reducing ROS at the source rather than driving output — conceptually the same rationale as membrane repair with phosphatidylcholine, but cardiolipin-specific. It lowers the danger signal rather than amplifying it. This is exactly why the sequence is SS-31 first: repair the membrane and quench ROS before anything asks the mitochondria to work harder. The traditional caution is preserved — it simply belongs on MOTS-c and NAD⁺, not on SS-31. **Two honest caveats: **Direct human trial evidence for SS-31 is in genetic mitochondrial disease, heart failure, and ischemia-reperfusion — not in CIRS/danger-state-specific randomized trials — so this approach is mechanistically grounded and clinically reasoned, but it is an extrapolation, and 2.5 mg is a deliberately conservative starting rung rather than a formally validated minimum effective dose. And even a repair agent can provoke a response in very sensitive patients — watch the response pattern, not the dose: if you see activation, agitation, or a crash even at 2.5 mg, slow to every-other-day or hold. Antioxidant cofactors (NAC, alpha-lipoic acid, CoQ10, glutathione, magnesium) should be on board before or alongside the start, since SS-31 repairs the membrane but the surrounding antioxidant network must be supplied to prevent re-oxidation. |\n| --- |\n\n### Evidence base\n\nStrong preclinical foundation (>200 published studies). Human trials: ReCLAIM (macular degeneration, Phase 1, 18 patients, 40 mg SC daily showed visual improvement of 3–6 letters); MMPOWER-3 (primary mitochondrial myopathy, missed primary endpoint but showed improvement on secondary patient-reported outcomes); ATTAIN/PROGRESS (Barth syndrome, ongoing). Cardiac trials in HFpEF showed improved diastolic function and 6-minute walk distance. The Stealth BioTherapeutics development program has advanced SS-31 further than any other mitochondrial-targeted peptide. Importantly, no serious adverse events have been reported across thousands of patient-exposures in trials.\n\n### Contraindications\n\n- Active malignancy (theoretical concern — improved mitochondrial function may support tumor cell metabolism; insufficient human data)\n\n- Pregnancy and lactation (no human data)\n\n- Known hypersensitivity to peptide compounds\n\n- Severe injection-site reaction history (rare with SS-31)\n\n### Drug interactions\n\nMinimal known drug interactions. SS-31 does not interact with cytochrome P450 enzymes, protein binding sites, or active drug transporters in clinically meaningful ways. Theoretical synergy with other mitochondrial agents (CoQ10, alpha-lipoic acid, NAD precursors, methylene blue) is generally favorable. Patients on metformin can use SS-31 — the combination supports mitochondrial function from different angles. Caution if combining with high-dose chemotherapy that targets mitochondrial pathways (e.g., certain anthracyclines), as the peptide's cardioprotective effect may interact with intended therapeutic mechanisms.\n\n### Baseline labs & monitoring\n\n- Baseline: comprehensive metabolic panel, CBC, hs-CRP, fasting insulin, glucose, lactate, optional 8-OHdG (oxidative DNA damage), GSH:GSSG ratio\n\n- Functional baselines: VO2 max or 6-minute walk if relevant, fatigue scale (FSS or similar), cognitive screen (MoCA in older patients)\n\n- Mid-cycle (week 2–3): symptom check, injection site assessment, energy/sleep diary\n\n- Post-cycle (week 4–6): repeat lactate (should drop), 8-OHdG (should drop), hs-CRP (should drop in inflammation-driven cases)\n\n- Long-term users: annual cancer screening per age-appropriate guidelines\n\n- Cardiac patients: echo at baseline and 3-month intervals\n\n### Side effects\n\n*Common (mild, dose-limiting only if severe):*\n\n- Mild injection site reaction (redness, transient nodule) — rotate sites, use abdomen or thigh\n\n- Transient headache in first 1–3 days — typically resolves\n\n- Mild GI upset uncommon\n\n- Transient fatigue paradox — some patients feel more tired in week 1 as mitophagy clears damaged mitochondria; resolves by week 2\n\n*Serious (stop and reassess):*\n\n- No serious adverse events reported in published trials at therapeutic doses\n\n- Rare anaphylaxis theoretically possible (any peptide)\n\n- If injection-site abscess or systemic symptoms develop, stop and reassess sourcing\n\n### Stacking & synergy\n\nSS-31 is the foundational peptide and should typically be started ALONE for the first 1–2 weeks before adding others — this allows assessment of tolerance and isolates response. After stabilization, layering options: (1) MOTS-c at week 2–3 to add metabolic activation once membranes are repaired ('repair the hardware before restarting the software'); (2) NAD⁺ at week 3–4 for sirtuin activation; (3) Humanin in cognitive decline / neurodegeneration — synergistic anti-apoptotic effect; (4) Glutathione, NAC, ALA, CoQ10 as nutritional adjuncts (always supplement these — they are consumed during ETC repair); (5) In CIRS, SS-31 can be started alongside binders (CSM/Welchol) without interference. AVOID stacking with strong oxidative stressors (ozone, high-dose IV vitamin C >25g) in the first 2 weeks — competes for the same redox bandwidth.\n\n### Clinical pearls\n\n- A key clinical principle: SS-31 is not an energy peptide — it is a damage-control peptide, which is why we keep it going during illness. In mold/CIRS patients who get acute viral illnesses, you down-titrate MOTS-c but KEEP SS-31 going.\n\n- The single most reliable mitochondrial peptide in current clinical practice — if your CIRS/Lyme/post-viral patient isn't responding to anything, start here.\n\n- The 'tired week' phenomenon is a positive sign, not a failure — it reflects mitophagy clearing damaged organelles. Counsel patients in advance.\n\n- Pairs exceptionally well with HBOT — the oxygen surge from HBOT generates ROS, which SS-31 immediately neutralizes at the membrane.\n\n- Ophthalmology research suggests topical/intravitreal SS-31 has efficacy in age-related macular degeneration. For now, systemic SQ remains standard.\n\n- In Barth syndrome and primary mitochondrial disease, SS-31 use should be coordinated with a mitochondrial specialist — these patients have unique trial-eligibility considerations.\n\n- Cost-conscious patients can use 5 mg every other day for maintenance once the initial 22-day cycle has produced response — extends each vial significantly.\n\n- Do not skip the antioxidant cofactors (NAC 600–1200 mg/day, ALA 300–600 mg/day, CoQ10 200–400 mg/day, glutathione 500 mg/day) — SS-31 repairs membranes but the broader antioxidant network must be supplied to prevent re-oxidation.\n\n- In post-MI or HFpEF patients on TRT or GH peptides, SS-31 should arguably be the FIRST added peptide — protects against reperfusion injury and supports cardiac mitochondrial recovery.\n\n- If a patient flares on SS-31 (very rare), suspect mast cell activation or sourcing problem. Verify COA. Consider 1/4 dose with mast cell pre-treatment (cromolyn, quercetin).\n\n| **MOTS-c**  —  *Mitochondrial-derived peptide; AMPK activator; metabolic flexibility restorer* |\n| --- |\n| **Vial** | **Dose** | **Route** | **Cycle** |\n| 10 mg / 1 mL | 5–10 mg | Subcutaneous | Every 5 days OR 2–3×/week × 4–12 weeks |\n\n### What it is\n\nMOTS-c (Mitochondrial Open Reading frame of the Twelve S rRNA-c) is a 16-amino-acid peptide encoded entirely within mitochondrial DNA — specifically within the 12S rRNA region. It is one of the very few peptides produced INSIDE the mitochondria, making it a 'mitochondrial-derived peptide' (MDP) along with humanin and the SHLP family. Discovered in 2015 by Pinchas Cohen's lab at USC, MOTS-c functions as a retrograde signaling molecule — sent FROM the mitochondria TO the nucleus to reprogram gene expression during metabolic stress. Levels decline with age, insulin resistance, obesity, and chronic illness. Exercise transiently raises endogenous MOTS-c, which is part of why exercise improves metabolic flexibility.\n\n### Mechanism of action\n\n- Activates AMPK (AMP-activated protein kinase) — the cellular 'fuel sensor' that switches metabolism toward fat oxidation, glucose uptake, and biogenesis.\n\n- Increases GLUT4 translocation in skeletal muscle → enhanced glucose disposal independent of insulin.\n\n- Suppresses lipogenesis (downregulates SREBP-1c, FASN) and promotes fatty acid oxidation (upregulates PPARα, CPT-1).\n\n- Stimulates mitochondrial biogenesis via PGC-1α — increases mitochondrial density per cell.\n\n- Enhances mitophagy — clears damaged mitochondria so new ones can take over.\n\n- Modulates the folate-methionine cycle through interactions with one-carbon metabolism — purine synthesis support.\n\n- Increases SOD2 (manganese superoxide dismutase) and catalase expression — improved antioxidant defense.\n\n- Improves insulin signaling at the receptor level — sensitizes muscle and liver to insulin.\n\n- Reduces hepatic gluconeogenesis — lowers fasting glucose in metabolic syndrome.\n\n- Crosses into the nucleus and binds DNA to alter gene transcription — true epigenetic modulator.\n\n### Clinical indications\n\n- Insulin resistance and type 2 diabetes (adjunct, not replacement for standard care)\n\n- Metabolic syndrome and visceral adiposity\n\n- Mitochondrial dysfunction in mold/mycotoxin illness (after SS-31 has stabilized membranes)\n\n- Post-viral fatigue, long COVID, post-Lyme syndromes\n\n- Sarcopenia and age-related metabolic decline\n\n- Athletic performance and recovery (caution: WADA-banned for competition)\n\n- NAFLD and metabolic-associated steatohepatitis\n\n- Adjunct in GLP-1 agonist therapy when insulin resistance persists\n\n- Cognitive decline with metabolic component (insulin-resistant brain)\n\n- Healthy aging / longevity programs\n\n### Dosing rationale & timing\n\nTwo main protocols are clinically used. The 'every 5 days' protocol (5–10 mg per dose, 6 injections per month) is based on the original Cohen lab pharmacokinetic data showing a long biological tail of effect. The '2–3× per week' protocol provides more consistent AMPK activation. Either works. Doses below 5 mg may be insufficient for AMPK activation in metabolically stressed patients. Doses above 10 mg per administration produce no additional benefit and may trigger reactive hypoglycemia in lean, insulin-sensitive patients. Timing: morning or pre-exercise dosing pairs naturally with AMPK's role in fuel switching — patients often report enhanced workout endurance. Empty stomach is unnecessary. The peptide must be cycled — continuous daily use leads to AMPK desensitization. Most cycles run 4–12 weeks with a 4-week washout before resumption.\n\n### Evidence base\n\nStrong preclinical foundation in metabolic disease models — type 2 diabetes, NAFLD, sarcopenia, aging. Human pilot studies are emerging but limited compared to SS-31. Endogenous MOTS-c levels correlate inversely with insulin resistance and positively with VO2 max. Exercise raises endogenous MOTS-c in humans (Reynolds et al., Nature Communications 2021). Polymorphisms in the MOTS-c sequence are associated with type 2 diabetes risk in Asian populations. Phase 1 human safety trials have been completed; larger efficacy trials in metabolic disease are ongoing. The peptide's mechanism is sufficiently well-validated that clinical use precedes definitive Phase 3 data — the typical 'translational gap.'\n\n### Contraindications\n\n- Active malignancy (caution — AMPK activation has complex effects on cancer; some tumors exploit it)\n\n- Pregnancy and lactation\n\n- Hypoglycemia-prone patients (T1DM, severe hypoglycemia history) — start at 5 mg with glucose monitoring\n\n- Folate deficiency without supplementation — MOTS-c targets de novo purine synthesis; folate deficiency can cause megaloblastic-pattern symptoms\n\n- Acute illness, infection, or fever — pause until resolved (raises metabolic demand)\n\n- Severe lean / underweight patients — risk of unwanted further weight loss\n\n### Drug interactions\n\nGenerally minimal pharmacologic interactions. Pharmacodynamic synergy with metformin (also AMPK activator) — combined use can cause additive glucose-lowering and may require diabetic medication adjustment. Synergy with GLP-1 agonists is favorable but monitor glucose in titration. Caution with insulin or sulfonylureas — risk of hypoglycemia compounds. SS-31 should precede MOTS-c by 2–4 weeks in mitochondrially-fragile patients. NAD⁺ and MOTS-c are complementary — different mechanisms, can stack. Resveratrol, berberine, and ALA also activate AMPK and may add to effect. Avoid combining with high-dose corticosteroids — opposing mechanisms. No CYP450 interactions.\n\n### Baseline labs & monitoring\n\n- Baseline: fasting glucose, fasting insulin, HOMA-IR, A1c, CMP, lipid panel, hs-CRP, lactate, B12/folate (especially methylfolate or 5-MTHF status)\n\n- Optional baseline: adiponectin, leptin, ApoB, Lp(a), GGT (mitochondrial/hepatic stress marker)\n\n- Functional: VO2 max if available, body composition (DEXA or InBody), waist circumference, fatigue scale\n\n- Mid-cycle (week 4–6): repeat fasting glucose, insulin, HOMA-IR, A1c if relevant, weight\n\n- Post-cycle (week 8–12): full metabolic panel, body composition, subjective energy/exercise tolerance\n\n- Long-term cycling patients: annual A1c, lipid panel, CRP, body comp, B12/folate\n\n### Side effects\n\n*Common (mild, dose-limiting only if severe):*\n\n- Mild reactive hypoglycemia in lean, sensitive patients in first 1–2 weeks (rare in T2DM patients; common in lean athletes)\n\n- Increased hunger or improved appetite — usually a positive signal of restored insulin sensitivity\n\n- Mild injection site reaction\n\n- Transient improved exercise tolerance with mild post-exercise muscle soreness\n\n- Increased urination if rapid fat oxidation produces ketone shift\n\n*Serious (stop and reassess):*\n\n- No serious adverse events reported at standard clinical doses\n\n- Theoretical concern: in folate-deficient patients, prolonged use without supplementation could exacerbate purine biosynthesis stress — supplement preventively\n\n- Rare anaphylaxis (any peptide)\n\n### Stacking & synergy\n\nMOTS-c is the second peptide in a sequenced mitochondrial protocol — start AFTER 2–4 weeks of SS-31 in fragile patients (mold, CIRS, Lyme). In healthier metabolic-syndrome patients, MOTS-c can be started alone or alongside SS-31 from day 1. Pairs exceptionally well with: (1) NAD⁺ — different mitochondrial mechanisms (membrane vs. sirtuin), additive effect; (2) GLP-1 agonists — addresses the residual insulin resistance many GLP-1 patients carry; (3) metformin — additive AMPK activation; (4) berberine — same direction; (5) Tesamorelin — visceral fat reduction synergy. AVOID stacking with: (1) high-dose corticosteroids; (2) acute infectious illness (pause). Sequence in mold protocols: SS-31 → MOTS-c → NAD⁺ → then thymic peptides → then anti-inflammatory peptides.\n\n### Clinical pearls\n\n- The same sequence applies: SS-31 repairs the hardware, MOTS-c restarts the software, NAD⁺ upgrades the operating system. This sequencing matters in fragile patients.\n\n- Pause MOTS-c during acute illness, fever, or major stressors — it raises metabolic demand and can worsen fatigue paradoxically. SS-31 stays on, MOTS-c pauses.\n\n- If a patient develops methylation-pattern symptoms (irritability, brain fog, fatigue) on MOTS-c, check folate status and add 5-MTHF 1–5 mg/day. The folate cycle interaction is real and underappreciated.\n\n- Lean athletes can experience reactive hypoglycemia in week 1 — start at 5 mg, dose post-meal initially, monitor symptoms.\n\n- WADA-banned for competition — flag this for any athlete patient who competes.\n\n- In GLP-1 microdosed patients with stalled progress, adding MOTS-c often breaks the plateau by addressing residual mitochondrial inflexibility.\n\n- Pairs well with continuous glucose monitoring — patients see the insulin-sensitivity improvement in real-time, which improves adherence.\n\n- OS-01 (oral pan-AMPK activator) shares overlapping mechanism — useful as oral bridge between MOTS-c cycles.\n\n- Most underused indication: post-Lyme metabolic exhaustion with normal labs but profound exercise intolerance. AMPK reactivation often unlocks recovery.\n\n- Do not mix in syringe with other peptides — concentration and pH considerations vary; inject separately even if at same visit.\n\n| **NAD⁺ (Nicotinamide Adenine Dinucleotide, subcutaneous)**  —  *Sirtuin co-substrate; redox cycle and DNA repair cofactor* |\n| --- |\n| **Vial** | **Dose** | **Route** | **Cycle** |\n| 1000 mg / 5 mL  (also 200 mg/mL concentrations) | 250–500 mg per injection | Subcutaneous (slow), IV, or intranasal | 2× per week × 8–12 weeks; or daily low-dose for fragile patients |\n\n### What it is\n\nNAD⁺ is not a peptide — it is a coenzyme present in every living cell, central to redox reactions and the substrate consumed by sirtuin enzymes (SIRT1–SIRT7), PARP enzymes (DNA repair), and CD38 (immune-related NAD consumption). NAD⁺ levels decline progressively with age — by age 50, levels are typically 50% of those at age 20. Chronic inflammation, alcohol, sedentary lifestyle, mold/mycotoxin illness, post-viral states, and chronic stress all accelerate NAD⁺ depletion. Subcutaneous NAD⁺ is a delivery method that bypasses oral bioavailability problems (oral NAD⁺ is largely degraded in the gut). Other delivery routes include IV (fastest and highest peak), intranasal (CNS-targeted), and oral precursors (NMN, NR, niacinamide).\n\n### Mechanism of action\n\n- Serves as the obligate co-substrate for sirtuin enzymes (SIRT1–7) — NAD-consuming deacetylases that regulate longevity, metabolism, inflammation, and DNA repair.\n\n- Required for PARP-1 activity in DNA single-strand break repair; PARP overactivation in chronic inflammation depletes NAD reserves.\n\n- Central electron carrier in oxidative phosphorylation (NADH → ETC Complex I) — directly powers ATP synthesis.\n\n- Substrate for CD38, the major NAD-consuming enzyme that rises with age and chronic inflammation; explains why anti-CD38 strategies (apigenin, quercetin) preserve NAD.\n\n- Modulates the folate-methylation cycle indirectly through SIRT-dependent regulation of one-carbon metabolism.\n\n- Activates SIRT1 → deacetylates p53, NF-κB, FOXO transcription factors → anti-inflammatory and pro-longevity gene expression.\n\n- Activates SIRT3 in mitochondria → deacetylates electron transport chain proteins and antioxidant enzymes (SOD2).\n\n- Restores cellular redox balance (NAD⁺/NADH ratio) — core determinant of metabolic flexibility.\n\n- Subcutaneous NAD⁺ produces sustained tissue levels with less of the acute flushing/discomfort of IV.\n\n### Clinical indications\n\n- Mitochondrial fatigue in mold/CIRS, Lyme, post-viral, long COVID\n\n- Cognitive decline, brain fog, age-related cognitive concerns\n\n- Addiction recovery (alcohol, opioid, stimulant) — IV NAD⁺ has best data\n\n- Aging and longevity programs (sirtuin support)\n\n- Chronic inflammation with high CD38 burden\n\n- DNA damage / oxidative stress (high 8-OHdG)\n\n- Athletic recovery and metabolic flexibility\n\n- Pre/post chemotherapy support (under oncology coordination)\n\n- Neurodegenerative disease support (Parkinson's, early AD, ALS)\n\n- Post-anesthesia cognitive recovery in older adults\n\n- Mitochondrial myopathies as adjunct to SS-31\n\n### Dosing rationale & timing\n\nSubcutaneous dosing typically uses 250–500 mg twice weekly, injected SLOWLY (over 60–90 seconds) to minimize the flushing reaction. Slow injection is critical — fast push produces facial flushing, transient chest pressure, and head pressure that scares patients but is harmless. Some clinicians divide a 500 mg dose into two 250 mg injections at separate sites. Daily low-dose (50–100 mg SC) is an alternative for very sensitive patients (CIRS, mast cell). IV NAD⁺ (250–1000 mg per infusion, 1–2 hours of slow drip) reaches higher peak levels but requires clinic time and dedicated IV access; reserved for severe deficits or addiction protocols. Intranasal NAD⁺ targets the CNS at lower systemic doses. Oral precursors (NMN 250–1000 mg/day, NR 300–600 mg/day) raise NAD⁺ but more slowly and modestly. INR2 (newer parenteral product) reportedly produces NAD elevation without the IV flushing reaction. Cycle 8–12 weeks then 4-week washout is standard; some patients tolerate continuous low-dose maintenance.\n\n### Evidence base\n\nStrong preclinical foundation — sirtuin biology, mitochondrial bioenergetics, DNA repair, longevity. Human RCT data is mixed but expanding: oral NAD precursors (NMN, NR) have multiple human RCTs showing modest improvements in walking distance, blood pressure, insulin sensitivity, and inflammatory markers. Parenteral NAD⁺ has less RCT data — primarily case series and observational reports from addiction medicine (Brazilian and US clinics) and longevity practices. Animal data robustly supports mitochondrial, neurologic, and metabolic benefits. Sinclair lab work has driven much of the public attention. Importantly, CD38 inhibition (apigenin, quercetin) may be as important as NAD supplementation for raising functional intracellular NAD — depending on the patient's CD38 burden.\n\n### Contraindications\n\n- Active untreated malignancy (theoretical — NAD supports cell proliferation; complex relationship with cancer biology)\n\n- Pregnancy and lactation\n\n- Severe cardiovascular disease, recent MI (relative — IV NAD only with cardiac monitoring)\n\n- Severe anxiety disorder (the flushing reaction can trigger panic in vulnerable patients)\n\n- Untreated MCAS without stabilization\n\n- Methylation-impaired patients without methyl-donor support — NAD metabolism increases methylation demand\n\n### Drug interactions\n\nNAD⁺ does not interact with most pharmaceuticals at the receptor level. Pharmacodynamic considerations: combining with statins is often beneficial (statins deplete CoQ10 and stress mitochondria; NAD⁺ supports recovery). Combining with metformin is favorable — both support AMPK-sirtuin axis. Caution with high-dose niacin (overlapping pathway, additive flushing). Methyl-donor depletion is a real concern: NAD methylation by NNMT consumes methyl groups; supplement methylfolate, B12, betaine, or TMG to prevent depletion-pattern symptoms (irritability, fatigue, hypersensitivity). Apigenin (50 mg/day) and quercetin (500 mg/day) inhibit CD38 and raise endogenous NAD — favorable adjuncts. Resveratrol activates SIRT1 directly — synergistic. Avoid combining with high-dose corticosteroids (suppress sirtuin signaling). Alcohol depletes NAD acutely — counsel reduction.\n\n### Baseline labs & monitoring\n\n- Baseline: CMP, CBC, hs-CRP, fasting glucose, A1c, lipid panel, B12, methylfolate (or homocysteine), 25-OH-D\n\n- Optional baseline: intracellular NAD level (Jinfiniti has a panel), 8-OHdG, GGT, NK cell function in immune-deficient patients\n\n- Functional: cognitive screen (MoCA), fatigue scale (FSS), exercise tolerance, sleep quality\n\n- Mid-cycle (week 4–6): symptom check, side effect review, methyl-donor adequacy\n\n- Post-cycle (week 8–12): repeat hs-CRP, fasting insulin, A1c if relevant, intracellular NAD if available\n\n- Longevity-protocol patients: AgingSOS panel quarterly (NAD + Klotho + senescence markers + oxidative stress)\n\n### Side effects\n\n*Common (mild, dose-limiting only if severe):*\n\n- Flushing reaction during fast injection — facial warmth, head pressure, mild chest tightness, brief shortness of breath. Resolves in 1–3 minutes if injection slowed or paused.\n\n- Mild nausea (especially IV)\n\n- Headache for 6–24 hours after first 1–2 doses\n\n- Insomnia if dosed late in the day (energizing)\n\n- Mild GI cramping\n\n- Vivid dreams\n\n- Increased thirst\n\n*Serious (stop and reassess):*\n\n- Severe flushing/panic reaction in very anxious patients — manage with slow injection, anti-anxiety strategies, switch to lower dose or alternative route\n\n- Rare anaphylaxis (any parenteral)\n\n- Methyl-donor depletion symptoms in chronic high-dose use (irritability, mood lability, fatigue) — supplement preventively\n\n- Acute decompensation in undiagnosed cardiac disease — rare, but baseline cardiac assessment in older patients with risk factors is prudent\n\n### Stacking & synergy\n\nNAD⁺ stacks well across nearly every protocol category. In mitochondrial sequencing: SS-31 → MOTS-c → NAD⁺. Pairs especially with: (1) Sirtuin activators — resveratrol 250–500 mg/day, pterostilbene; (2) CD38 inhibitors — apigenin 50 mg/day, quercetin 500 mg/day, fisetin 100 mg/day (these are arguably as important as NAD itself); (3) Methyl donors — methylfolate 1–2 mg/day, methylcobalamin 1000 mcg/day, TMG 500–1000 mg/day, betaine; (4) Senolytics — D+Q pulse protocols benefit from NAD support; (5) Klotho-raising protocols; (6) GH peptides — NAD supports the increased metabolic demand. AVOID combining with: high-dose corticosteroids (chronic), unmanaged anxiety states (until stabilized), uncoordinated use during active chemotherapy. In addiction medicine, NAD IV is often combined with B-complex and amino acid IVs.\n\n### Clinical pearls\n\n- The flushing reaction is dose-rate-dependent, not dose-dependent. Slow the injection, not the dose. Most patients tolerate 500 mg SC over 90 seconds without significant reaction.\n\n- Oral NAD does NOT raise intracellular NAD effectively — this point deserves emphasis. NMN raises intracellular NAD better than NR or oral NAD.\n\n- CD38 is the under-recognized variable. Two patients on identical NAD doses can have wildly different intracellular levels because of CD38 burden. Apigenin + quercetin are essential adjuncts.\n\n- INR2 is a newer product reportedly producing NAD elevation without the typical IV reaction — 40-minute drip, induces endogenous NAD production. Worth tracking as availability expands.\n\n- In addiction recovery, IV NAD doses can run as high as 1000+ mg over 6–8 hours daily for 5–10 days — these protocols are specialty-clinic territory.\n\n- Mast cell patients can flare on NAD — start at 50 mg SC and titrate slowly. Premedicate with quercetin or cromolyn.\n\n- Cancer history is nuanced: most longevity-medicine clinicians use NAD in cancer survivors who are years out of remission; active disease is generally a contraindication. Coordinate with oncology.\n\n- Pairs spectacularly with HBOT — both raise mitochondrial efficiency from different angles. Many CIRS patients respond to this combination when nothing else has moved the needle.\n\n- Patients should not dose NAD after 2 PM — universally too stimulating for sleep.\n\n- Intranasal NAD is underused for cognitive applications — produces measurable cognitive benefit at lower systemic doses.\n\n- Don't ignore the basics while adding NAD: alcohol cessation, B-vitamin sufficiency, sleep optimization, and exercise raise endogenous NAD more than any injection.\n\n- Methyl-donor depletion is real and underrecognized. If a patient on high-dose NAD becomes irritable or fatigued after 4–6 weeks, suspect methylation depletion before assuming NAD failure.\n\n# Mitochondrial Category — Quick Reference Appendix\n\n## Sequencing summary\n\n| **Order** | **Peptide** | **Phase Goal** | **Pause if…** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| **1** | **SS-31 (Elamipretide)** | Membrane repair / ROS control | Severe injection-site reaction (rare); active malignancy |\n| **2** | **MOTS-c** | Metabolic activation (AMPK) | Acute illness, fever, severe stress; folate deficiency |\n| **3** | **NAD⁺** | Sirtuin / DNA repair / redox | Methyl-donor depletion symptoms; severe anxiety; active cancer |\n\n## The mitochondrial 'support stack' — supplements that should always run alongside\n\n- NAC 600–1200 mg/day (glutathione precursor)\n\n- Alpha-lipoic acid 300–600 mg/day (regenerates other antioxidants)\n\n- CoQ10 / Ubiquinol 200–400 mg/day (ETC cofactor)\n\n- Liposomal glutathione 500 mg/day (or IV)\n\n- Magnesium glycinate 300–600 mg/day\n\n- B-complex with methylated forms (methylfolate, methylcobalamin)\n\n- CD38 inhibitors with NAD⁺: apigenin 50 mg/day + quercetin 500 mg/day\n\n- Sirtuin activators with NAD⁺: resveratrol or pterostilbene 250–500 mg/day\n\n## Common mistakes to avoid\n\n- Starting MOTS-c before SS-31 stabilizes membranes — produces flares in fragile patients\n\n- Continuing MOTS-c during acute illness — worsens fatigue, raises metabolic demand\n\n- High-dose NAD without methyl-donor support — depletes methylation capacity, causes mood lability and fatigue at week 4–6\n\n- Forgetting the antioxidant cofactors — peptides cannot work without the broader network\n\n- Using oral NAD instead of NMN or parenteral — oral NAD does not raise intracellular NAD effectively\n\n- Ignoring CD38 burden — high CD38 patients need apigenin/quercetin or NAD effects are blunted\n\n- Pushing harder when patient flares — almost always a sign to slow down, not accelerate\n\n*Disclaimer: Educational reference for licensed practitioners. Doses are starting suggestions; adjust per individual response, comorbidities, labs, and state scope of practice. Coordinate with specialists for cancer history, severe cardiovascular disease, and pregnancy.*\n\n**Addendum — Managing the Protocol During Illness, Injury ****&**** Recovery**\n\nThe mitochondrial rebuild is not linear. It is adaptive: when an acute stressor arrives, priorities change, and the protocol changes with them. The governing idea is simple — **during illness or injury the goal shifts from building energy to protecting mitochondria and controlling inflammation.** Do not abruptly stop the protocol unless the patient is clearly intolerant; modify intensity and sequencing instead.\n\n| **The one-line mental model** **SS-31 protects the mitochondria. MOTS-c builds capacity. GHK-Cu repairs structure. Epitalon programs longevity. **You earn the right to move forward — you do not ask a depleted system to regenerate until it can sustain regeneration. |\n| --- |\n\n**If acute illness arises (viral flare, infection, Herx, surgery)**\n\n**Pause or down-titrate**\n\n- **MOTS-c — **AMPK activation raises metabolic demand; during illness this can worsen fatigue, hunger, or inflammatory symptoms.\n\n- **GHK-Cu — **stimulates repair, collagen turnover, and copper utilization; during acute inflammation it often feels “too activating.”\n\n- **Epitalon — **pushes circadian and telomeric signaling; best reserved for when energy reserves exist.\n\n**Maintain or prioritize**\n\n- **SS-31 — **keep on board whenever possible. It repairs the inner mitochondrial membrane, reduces ROS, and protects mitochondria during inflammatory stress. SS-31 is not an energy peptide — it is a damage-control peptide, which is exactly why it stays during illness.\n\n- **ARA-290 — **supports endothelial mitochondria, reduces microvascular inflammation; helpful in pain, neuropathy, and autonomic flares.\n\n- **VIP (if already started) — **supports immune regulation and neuroinflammation; often stabilizing during setbacks.\n\n**If the stressor is injury (MSK, surgery, trauma)**\n\nStrategy shifts from energy to repair.\n\n- **Continue SS-31.**\n\n- Add or emphasize **BPC-157 and TB-500** for tissue repair.\n\n- **Temporarily reduce MOTS-c** until pain and inflammation are controlled — inflammation and tissue repair already raise ATP demand, and pushing biogenesis too early worsens fatigue and appetite dysregulation.\n\n**Why GHK-Cu and Epitalon are added only after energy returns**\n\n**GHK-Cu — why it waits**\n\nGHK-Cu signals tissue repair, activates collagen synthesis, supports vascular remodeling, and enhances mitochondrial gene expression — but it requires adequate ATP, functional copper handling, and stable redox balance. Added too early, patients report fatigue, hunger, a “wired but tired” state, or inflammatory flares. That is not intolerance — it is poor readiness. GHK-Cu is not for broken engines; it is for engines that can now rebuild parts.\n\n**Epitalon — why it waits even longer**\n\nEpitalon drives telomerase signaling, pineal–circadian regulation, and anti-senescence pathways — it assumes stable mitochondria, adequate NAD/NADH balance, and a functional circadian rhythm. Added too early it can cause insomnia, anxiety, paradoxical fatigue, or “over-signaling” symptoms. Epitalon is not restorative — it is directive, and the system must be ready to receive direction.\n\n**How to tell a patient is ready to advance**\n\nA patient is ready to add GHK-Cu, then Epitalon, when most of the following hold — stable, not just briefly improved:\n\n- Energy is more stable, with less post-exertional crash\n\n- Hunger has normalized (not excessive)\n\n- Sleep is deeper or more consistent\n\n- Brain fog has improved\n\n- Reduced pain or autonomic symptoms\n\n*Supporting labs where available: improving inflammatory markers, stabilizing cytokine patterns, improved mitochondrial markers, and better glucose handling.*\n\n**Adjustment algorithm at a glance**\n\n| **If illness or injury occurs** | **When recovery resumes** |\n| --- | --- |\n| Keep SS-31 | Restart MOTS-c at a lower dose |\n| Consider ARA-290 (and BPC-157 / TB-500 for injury) | Observe tolerance 1–2 weeks |\n| Pause or reduce MOTS-c | Add GHK-Cu once readiness criteria are met |\n| Delay GHK-Cu and Epitalon | Add Epitalon last |\n\n*The protocol is adaptive, not linear — it changes with stress, illness, and recovery. When in doubt during a flare, slow down rather than push harder; pushing through a flare is almost always the wrong move.*","locked":false,"chars":42444},{"kind":"vol","code":"02","title":"Thymic & Immune","text":"","locked":true,"chars":47426},{"kind":"vol","code":"03","title":"Anti-inflammatory & Mucosal","text":"","locked":true,"chars":56151},{"kind":"vol","code":"04","title":"Antimicrobial","text":"","locked":true,"chars":42042},{"kind":"vol","code":"05","title":"GH Axis","text":"**Peptides, Practiced. — Beyond the Course**\n**Peptide Teaching Edition**\n*Volume 5 — GH Axis Peptides*\nSermorelin  ·  CJC-1295 + Ipamorelin  ·  Tesamorelin\nProvider Reference  |  v1.0\n| **How to use this volume** Three peptides anchor this category. They are NOT interchangeable — each is optimized for a different patient phenotype. Choosing correctly significantly affects clinical outcomes and side effect risk. Sermorelin: the older patient, mild GH decline, sleep restoration, gentle effect, lowest cost. CJC-1295 + Ipamorelin: the middle-aged patient, body composition, recovery, athletic application, the workhorse of age-management. Tesamorelin: the metabolic patient with visceral adiposity or NAFLD, cognitive concerns, the strongest evidence base. *All three GH peptides PRESERVE the body's natural negative feedback — they amplify endogenous GH release rather than replacing it. This makes them fundamentally safer than exogenous human growth hormone (HGH).* |\n| --- |\n# GH Axis Peptides — Category Overview\nThe growth hormone axis is one of the most clinically important systems in age-management and chronic-illness recovery. Endogenous GH production peaks in adolescence and declines progressively from the third decade onward — by age 60, most adults have only 25-50% of their peak GH output. This decline is not benign: it correlates with sarcopenia, visceral fat accumulation, declining bone density, deteriorating sleep architecture, slower wound healing, decreased exercise tolerance, mood changes, and accelerated aging in general.\n\n### Also inside this section…\n\nChoosing among the three GH peptides depends on the patient's clinical phenotype: — | **The GH peptide decision tree** **Question 1: Is the dominant problem visceral adiposity, NAFLD, or metabolic syndrome with visceral fat?** → YES: Tesamoreli\n\nWhat it is — Sermorelin is a synthetic 29-amino-acid peptide identical to the first 29 amino acids of endogenous growth hormone-releasing hormone (GHRH) — also known as GRF ","locked":true,"snippet":true,"chars":54229},{"kind":"vol","code":"06","title":"GLP & Metabolic","text":"**Peptides, Practiced. — Beyond the Course**\n**Peptide Teaching Edition**\n*Volume 6 — GLP / Metabolic Peptides*\nTirzepatide-class (GLP-T)  ·  Retatrutide  ·  Muscle Preservation Stack\nProvider Reference  |  v1.0\n| **How to use this volume** This volume covers the GLP-1 class — currently the most powerful pharmacologic intervention available for obesity, T2DM, NAFLD, and metabolic syndrome. The clinical question for most practitioners is no longer 'should I use GLP-1s?' but 'how do I use them WITHOUT producing sarcopenic obesity, hair loss, skin laxity, and bone density loss?' *The dedicated MUSCLE PRESERVATION STACK section in this volume is the most clinically important content. Every patient on a GLP-1 should have an active preservation protocol. Reading this volume without applying the preservation stack produces predictable bad outcomes.* Semaglutide is briefly mentioned but not extensively covered — most modern functional medicine practice has moved to tirzepatide-class as the primary GLP-1, with retatrutide for refractory cases. |\n| --- |\n# GLP / Metabolic Peptides — Category Overview\nThe GLP-1 class has fundamentally changed metabolic medicine. Patients who have failed every previous intervention — diet, exercise, behavioral therapy, bariatric surgery in some cases — achieve and maintain meaningful weight loss with GLP-1s. Type 2 diabetes is now treatable to remission in many cases. NAFLD is reversible. Cardiovascular outcomes improve. The question facing the modern functional medicine practitioner is not whether GLP-1s should be used, but how to use them while preserving muscle, bone, skin, and hair.\n\n### Also inside this section…\n\nThe fundamental clinical reality of GLP-1 class agents: — | **What GLP-1s do without intervention** **Without a preservation stack, GLP-1 patients lose:** • 25-40% of weight loss as LEAN MASS (muscle and bone) — sarcop\n\nThe Muscle Preservation Stack on GLP-1 Therapy — ***The most clinically important content in this volume. This stack is not optional — it is the difference between weight loss and sarcopenic obesity.***","locked":true,"snippet":true,"chars":83616},{"kind":"vol","code":"07","title":"Tissue Repair","text":"**Peptides, Practiced. — Beyond the Course**\n**Peptide Teaching Edition**\n*Volume 7 — Tissue Repair Peptides*\nTB-500 (Thymosin Beta-4)  ·  GHK-Cu  ·  Wolverine Combination\nProvider Reference  |  v1.0\n| **How to use this volume** Three peptides anchor this category. They are foundational to orthopedic, post-surgical, regenerative medicine, and skin/hair applications. TB-500 provides actin remodeling and stem cell mobilization; GHK-Cu provides collagen synthesis and broad gene modulation; Wolverine combines BPC-157 + TB-500 for major tissue injury. BPC-157 (covered in Volume 3 — Anti-inflammatory / Mucosal) is the third member of the tissue repair workhorse trio. Cross-reference Volume 3 for full BPC-157 details. *Skin and hair applications of GHK-Cu are detailed further in Volume 11 (Skin and Hair Synthesis) — the present volume covers GHK-Cu's full mechanism and dosing, with cross-references forward to indication-specific protocols.* |\n| --- |\n# Tissue Repair Peptides — Category Overview\nTissue repair is one of the most clinically valuable application areas for peptide medicine. The body's intrinsic repair capacity is finite — declining with age, blunted in chronic illness, overwhelmed by major injury. Peptides provide targeted amplification of repair signaling that can dramatically accelerate healing and improve outcomes.\n\n### Also inside this section…\n\nTB-500 (Thymosin Beta-4) is the actin-sequestering and stem cell mobilizing peptide. Its primary value is in indications where cellular migration and stem cell recruitment matter most — tendon and ligament injury, cardiac repair, hair regrowth, and complex chronic wound healing. The Goldstein lab provenance (same researcher as Thymosin Alpha-1) gives it substantial scientific credibility. — GHK-Cu is the gene modulation and collagen synthesis peptide. With approximately 4,000+ genes modulated by GHK-Cu, it is one of the most epigenetically active s\n\n| **The category decision tree** **Question 1: What is the dominant clinical problem?** → Skin aging, scarring, wound healing, hair loss: GHK-Cu first-line (topical, microneedling, or systemic depending on indication). → Tendon/ligament injury or post-surgical recovery: TB-500 alone for moderate injury; Wolverine (BPC-157 + TB-500) for major injury. → Gut barrier, microvascular dysfunction, chronic gastritis: BPC-157 (Volume 3) is the primary peptide; TB-500 may add stem cell support in complex cases. **Question 2: How severe is the injury?** → Minor (simple tendinitis, mild sprain): single peptide adequate (BPC-157 or TB-500 individually). → Moderate (significant tendinopathy, post-surgical recovery without major repair): single peptide with regional dosing typically adequate. → Major (partial tendon tear, post-surgical major repair, refractory chronic injury): Wolverine combination justified. | — | --- |","locked":true,"snippet":true,"chars":59705},{"kind":"vol","code":"08","title":"Cognitive, Neuro & HPG","text":"","locked":true,"chars":112646},{"kind":"vol","code":"09","title":"Gut","text":"","locked":true,"chars":18788},{"kind":"vol","code":"10","title":"Klotho & Longevity Protein","text":"KLOTHO — Clinical Reference\n**Peptides, Practiced. — Beyond the Course**\n**Teaching Volume 10**\n**KLOTHO**\n*The Longevity Protein*\nA Comprehensive Clinical Reference for Functional & Regenerative Medicine Practitioners\n*Mechanism · Measurement · Modulation · Therapeutics*\n*A clinical synthesis of Klotho biology and its translation to longevity practice.*\n# **Table of Contents**\n\n### Also inside this section…\n\n6.  Clinical Conditions Linked to Low Klotho — 7.  When Klotho is Too High — The Cancer Caveat\n\n16. Cautions, Contraindications, and Pitfalls — 17. Patient Education Talking Points","locked":true,"snippet":true,"chars":47006},{"kind":"protocol","code":"P01","title":"Concussion & Head Injury","text":"","locked":true,"chars":10562},{"kind":"protocol","code":"P02","title":"Systemic Anti-Aging & Longevity","text":"","locked":true,"chars":9841},{"kind":"protocol","code":"P03","title":"Mold, Mycotoxin & Environmental Illness","text":"Peptides, Practiced. — Beyond the Course  |  Mold, Mycotoxin & Environmental Illness\n**Peptides, Practiced. — Beyond the Course**\n**Mold, Mycotoxin ****&**** Environmental Illness**\n*A Sequenced Peptide Protocol for Recovery from CIRS, Mycotoxin Illness, and Chronic Environmental Toxicity*\n*Scenario protocol · Four-phase framework with inline dosing · Cross-references the clinical volumes · Clinician reference*\n| **The governing principle** **Repair the terrain before you push the system. **Mold injures the mitochondria, the immune system, the gut, the vasculature, and the autonomic nerves. Peptides correct these upstream, which is what finally lets detox, binders, and antimicrobials work. Sequence matters more than the list: stabilize the engine and the wiring first, rebuild immunity second, clear infection third, maintain fourth. Run out of order — especially antimicrobials before the immune system and mitochondria are ready — and sensitive patients flare. |\n| --- |\n# **1. Why early peptide intervention**\nMold and mycotoxin illness, chronic environmental toxicity, and post-infectious syndromes create a combination of mitochondrial collapse, sustained cytokine activation, and immune dysregulation that detoxification alone cannot correct. When mitochondrial weakness persists beyond roughly three to six months, the picture entrenches: mitochondria stiffen and lose responsiveness, cells shift toward glycolysis and chronic fatigue, ER stress and protein misfolding add immune burden, T-regulatory function falters, and histamine/MCAS patterns worsen.\n\n### Also inside this section…\n\n**2. Phase 1 (Weeks 0–4) — Immune reboot, endothelial repair, mitochondrial first aid** — Goal: reset immune architecture, repair the vasculature, restore gut and sinus barriers, and stabilize mitochondria so later therapies (MOTS-c, LL-37) can work \n\n- **Indication: **low GM-CSF, low innate markers, immune collapse from chronic toxicity, immune aging. — - **Mechanism: **restores thymic epithelial function, normalizes Th1/Th2 and IL-2/IFN-γ, rebuilds bone-marrow signaling and innate recruitment.","locked":true,"snippet":true,"chars":17019},{"kind":"protocol","code":"P04","title":"Chronic Viral Reactivation","text":"","locked":true,"chars":31634},{"kind":"protocol","code":"P05","title":"Gut Rebuild Protocol","text":"","locked":true,"chars":8390},{"kind":"special","code":"B01","title":"Regenerative Medicine Primer","text":"","locked":true,"chars":14195},{"kind":"special","code":"E01","title":"Low-Dose GLP-1 for Inflammation","text":"","locked":true,"chars":22516},{"kind":"special","code":"REF","title":"Dose & Indication Reference","text":"","locked":true,"chars":8238},{"kind":"special","code":"REG","title":"Regulatory & Prescribing Landscape","text":"","locked":true,"chars":12809},{"kind":"special","code":"LABS","title":"Peptide Monitoring Labs","text":"","locked":true,"chars":4450},{"kind":"special","code":"CITE","title":"References & Citations","text":"","locked":true,"chars":171847}];
